WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,056
A principal is an identity that can request access to a cloud resource or API.

2
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:17,458
Common building blocks include users, groups, roles, service accounts, managed identities, workload identities, applications, API keys, access tokens, and temporary credentials.

3
00:00:17,458 --> 00:00:20,301
Different providers use different names.

4
00:00:20,301 --> 00:00:33,730
AWS roles, Azure managed identities, and Google Cloud service accounts are not identical, but they all represent a basic idea: something is being authenticated and then authorized to perform actions.

5
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:38,391
Human identities and non-human identities need different controls.

6
00:00:38,391 --> 00:00:48,344
A person may sign in through a browser or command-line tool using a federated identity provider, multi-factor authentication, and role assumption.

7
00:00:48,344 --> 00:00:57,587
A workload may run inside a virtual machine, container, serverless function, Kubernetes pod, CI/CD pipeline, or deployment automation.

8
00:00:57,587 --> 00:01:05,328
It may need to read a secret, write logs, publish an artifact, deploy infrastructure, or call another cloud service.

9
00:01:05,328 --> 00:01:09,436
The security design should reflect the identity type and job.

10
00:01:09,436 --> 00:01:14,965
Every identity should have a purpose, owner, lifecycle, and permission boundary.

11
00:01:14,965 --> 00:01:17,098
Purpose explains why it exists.

12
00:01:17,098 --> 00:01:23,497
Ownership explains who reviews it, approves changes, and responds when something looks wrong.

13
00:01:23,497 --> 00:01:28,710
Lifecycle explains when it should be created, rotated, disabled, or deleted.

14
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:37,321
A permission boundary or equivalent guardrail limits what the identity can do, even if a policy or role assignment changes later.

15
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:41,744
These details prevent stale identities from becoming quiet risk.

16
00:01:41,744 --> 00:01:43,482
Credentials also differ.

17
00:01:43,482 --> 00:01:51,302
Long-lived access keys and static secrets are operationally risky because they can be copied, forgotten, or leaked.

18
00:01:51,302 --> 00:02:01,809
Temporary credentials, managed identity mechanisms, workload identity federation, and short-lived tokens reduce that risk when implemented correctly.

19
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:04,731
The goal is not merely to create identities.

20
00:02:04,731 --> 00:02:12,315
The goal is to make each identity understandable, owned, scoped, auditable, and removable when its purpose ends.

21
00:02:12,315 --> 00:02:15,949
Human identities usually follow people and job roles.

22
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:21,083
They need onboarding, role changes, offboarding, MFA, and periodic review.

23
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:24,717
Non-human identities follow systems and automation.

24
00:02:24,717 --> 00:02:32,932
They need owners, rotation or short-lived credentials, clear deployment context, and removal when the workload retires.

25
00:02:32,932 --> 00:02:43,043
Treating both categories the same leads to mistakes: humans get permanent machine keys, or workloads inherit human-style administrator privileges.

26
00:02:43,043 --> 00:02:44,781
Naming and metadata help.

27
00:02:44,781 --> 00:02:51,812
An identity name, tag, label, description, or owner field should tell a reviewer what the identity is for.

28
00:02:51,812 --> 00:03:00,343
If a service account called temp-admin has existed for two years and no one knows what it runs, that is not only untidy; it is a risk.

29
00:03:00,343 --> 00:03:06,347
Identity inventory is the foundation for access review, incident response, and cleanup.

30
00:03:06,347 --> 00:03:10,533
Reviews should ask whether an identity still matches reality.

31
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:13,219
Does the group still represent the team?

32
00:03:13,219 --> 00:03:16,063
Does the role still match the application?

33
00:03:16,063 --> 00:03:17,880
Is the API key still needed?

34
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,171
Is the token issuer still trusted?

35
00:03:20,171 --> 00:03:29,571
IAM drift often comes from identities that were correct when created but were never revisited after projects, teams, and workloads changed.
